      "keyNotes": [
        "Row 4/4/0 (tetrahedral) is the worked example at the top, so the table has 12 rows; score out of 12.",
        "Lone pairs = domains − bonding pairs. The electron geometry depends only on the number of domains.",
        "Angles are textbook values. With lone pairs, PhET's Model screen shows angles a little smaller than ideal (lone pairs repel more), so accept close values marked “less than”.",
        "Rows with 2 bonding pairs come out Bent (3 or 4 domains) or Linear (5 domains): good for discussion.",
        "Shape names match PhET's own labels (e.g. Seesaw, T-shaped, Square pyramidal).",
        "Sketches: any drawing with the right number of atoms and lone pairs in the right arrangement earns the point; wedges and dashes are a bonus, not required."
      ]
